-- guide · 10 Steps to a Sale
INSERT INTO cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources (type, title, body, url, category, metaJson, sortOrder, isPublished, featured) SELECT 'guide', '10 Steps to a Sale', '# 10 Steps to a Sale\n## Your Cabinet Dealz Affiliate Sales Playbook\n\n> Affiliates who follow this 10-step system consistently report a **35% increase in sales** compared to those who rely on word-of-mouth alone. These steps are not theory — they are the proven actions that turn conversations into commissions.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Prospect — Find Your Next Customer\n\nEvery sale starts with a conversation. Your best prospects are people who are already thinking about their kitchen.\n\n**Where to find them:**\n- Homeowners posting in local Facebook groups about remodeling\n- Contractors and builders who need a reliable cabinet source for their clients\n- Real estate agents staging homes for sale (kitchens sell houses)\n- Interior designers looking for a trade partner\n- Neighbors, friends, and family — your warm market is your fastest market\n- Home improvement stores — people browsing cabinet displays are actively shopping\n- New construction neighborhoods — knock on doors during the build phase\n\n**Your goal at this step:** Get a name, a phone number, and a rough idea of their timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Make Contact — Introduce Yourself and Your Store\n\nYour first contact sets the tone. Keep it simple, friendly, and focused on them — not on cabinets.\n\n**Text script:**\n> "Hey [Name], this is [Your Name] with [Your Store Name]. I help homeowners get beautiful kitchen cabinets at contractor pricing — without the contractor markup. Do you have 5 minutes this week to take a look at what I offer?"\n\n**Phone script:**\n> "Hi [Name], I''m [Your Name] — I run a cabinet store called [Your Store Name]. I work with homeowners who are remodeling their kitchens and want quality cabinets at a fair price. Are you or anyone you know thinking about updating their kitchen?"\n\n**Key rule:** Always end with a question. Questions keep the conversation going.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Qualify — Ask the Right Questions\n\nBefore you invest time in a prospect, make sure they are a real buyer. A qualified lead has a timeline, a budget, and a decision to make.\n\n**The four qualifying questions:**\n1. "What''s your timeline — are you hoping to start in the next 30, 60, or 90 days?"\n2. "Do you have a rough budget in mind for the cabinets?"\n3. "Is this a full kitchen replacement or are you updating specific areas?"\n4. "Are you working with a contractor, or doing the installation yourself?"\n\nA prospect who says "sometime next year, not sure on budget" is a long-term nurture. A prospect who says "we want to start in 6 weeks and have $8,000 for cabinets" is a hot lead — move fast.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Share Your Link — Send Them to Your Storefront\n\nOnce you have a qualified prospect, send them your storefront link immediately. This is your 24/7 showroom.\n\n**Text to send:**\n> "Here''s my store — you can browse all the door styles and see pricing right on the site: [your storefront URL]. The design tool lets you plan your whole kitchen layout. Let me know what styles catch your eye and I''ll put together a quote."\n\n**Why this works:** Your storefront is branded to you, shows your prices, and has the design tool built in. It does the selling while you sleep.\n\n---\n\n## Step 5: Walk the Design Tool — Show Them How to Plan Their Kitchen\n\nThe 2D kitchen design tool is your most powerful sales asset. Customers who use it are far more likely to buy because they can see their kitchen before they commit.\n\n**How to use it as a sales tool:**\n- Offer to do a screen share or in-person demo\n- Start with their actual kitchen dimensions (have them measure first — see the Kitchen Design Fundamentals guide)\n- Place the cabinets together and show them how the layout works\n- Let them pick door styles and see the room scene photos\n- Save the design and use it as the basis for their quote\n\n**Script:** "Let me show you how the design tool works — it takes about 10 minutes and at the end you''ll have a real layout and a price. Want to do that now or schedule a time?"\n\n---\n\n## Step 6: Present the Quote — Walk Through Pricing Clearly\n\nOnce the design is done, the quote is built from it. Walk the customer through it line by line.\n\n**How to present:**\n- Start with the total: "Your complete kitchen in [door style] comes to $X."\n- Break it down by section: uppers, lowers, tall cabinets, specialty pieces\n- Remind them what''s included: all boxes, doors, hardware, soft-close hinges\n- Address shipping: "Shipping is calculated based on your ZIP code and will be added at checkout."\n\n**Never apologize for the price.** Your pricing is competitive with big box stores but the quality is significantly better. If they push back, go to Step 7.\n\n---\n\n## Step 7: Handle Objections — Turn "No" Into "Not Yet"\n\nEvery sale has objections. The customer who objects is still engaged — they just need more information or reassurance.\n\n| Objection | Response |\n|---|---|\n| "It''s too expensive." | "Compared to what? Let me show you what the same kitchen costs at [big box store]. Our price is usually 20–40% less for better quality." |\n| "I need to think about it." | "Of course — what''s the main thing you want to think through? I want to make sure you have everything you need to decide." |\n| "I''m not sure about the quality." | "That''s a fair question. Our cabinets are [all-plywood construction / dovetail joints / soft-close standard]. Would it help to see some photos of installed kitchens?" |\n| "I want to get other quotes." | "Absolutely — you should. When you get them, compare the box construction (plywood vs. particleboard) and what''s included. I''m confident we''ll be competitive." |\n| "I need to talk to my spouse." | "Of course. Would it help if I sent you a summary you could share with them? Or we could do a quick call together." |\n\n---\n\n## Step 8: Close — Ask for the Order\n\nMost salespeople lose the sale because they never ask for it. Closing is simply asking the customer to make a decision.\n\n**Simple closes:**\n- "Are you ready to move forward? I can get your order placed today."\n- "If the pricing works for you, the next step is just a deposit to lock in your order. Want to do that now?"\n- "What would it take for you to feel comfortable moving forward today?"\n\n**The deposit close:** "We require a 50% deposit to begin processing your order. The balance is due before shipping. Want me to send you the payment link?"\n\nIf they say yes — stop talking and process the order. If they say no — ask what''s stopping them and go back to Step 7.\n\n---\n\n## Step 9: Follow Up — Never Let a Lead Go Cold\n\nMost sales happen on the 5th to 12th contact. Most salespeople give up after 2. Your CRM is your follow-up system — use it.\n\n**The follow-up cadence:**\n- **Day 1:** Send storefront link + design tool invitation\n- **Day 3:** Check in — "Did you get a chance to look at the site? Any questions?"\n- **Day 7:** Share a room scene photo or a customer story — "Just wanted to show you what [door style] looks like installed…"\n- **Day 14:** "Just following up — are you still planning to update your kitchen?"\n- **Day 30:** "Checking in — any updates on your timeline? We have some new styles you might like."\n- **Day 60:** "Still here when you''re ready. Kitchen projects often take longer to start than expected — no pressure."\n\n**Key rule:** Always add a note to your CRM after every contact. If you don''t write it down, it didn''t happen.\n\n---\n\n## Step 10: Get Referrals — Your Best Leads Come From Happy Customers\n\nAfter every successful installation, ask for referrals. A happy customer is your best marketing.\n\n**Script (send by text 2–3 weeks after delivery):**\n> "Hey [Name], I hope the kitchen is coming together beautifully! If you know anyone else who''s thinking about updating their kitchen, I''d love an introduction. I''ll take great care of them — and there''s a thank-you gift in it for you."\n\n**Make it easy:** Give them your digital business card link to forward. One text from a happy customer is worth more than 10 cold calls.\n\n---\n\n## Your 35% Advantage\n\nAffiliates who consistently apply all 10 steps — prospecting daily, following up systematically, using the design tool, and asking for referrals — report **35% higher sales** than those who rely on passive referrals alone. The difference is not talent. It is process.\n\nYour storefront is live. Your tools are ready. Now go find your next customer.\n\n---\n\n*Cabinet Dealz Affiliate Program — cabinetdealz.com*\n', NULL, 'sales', NULL, 200, TRUE, FALSE FROM DUAL WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources WHERE type = 'guide' AND title = '10 Steps to a Sale');
-- guide · Kitchen Design Fundamentals
INSERT INTO cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources (type, title, body, url, category, metaJson, sortOrder, isPublished, featured) SELECT 'guide', 'Kitchen Design Fundamentals', '# Kitchen Design Fundamentals\n## A Practical Guide for Cabinet Dealz Affiliates and Their Customers\n\n> Understanding the basics of kitchen design helps you guide customers confidently, avoid costly mistakes, and close more sales. Share this guide with every customer before they start planning.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: How to Measure Your Kitchen\n\nAccurate measurements are the foundation of every successful cabinet order. Mistakes in measurement lead to gaps, overhangs, or cabinets that simply do not fit.\n\n### What You Need\n- A metal tape measure (cloth tapes stretch and give inaccurate readings)\n- Graph paper or the Cabinet Dealz 2D design tool\n- A pencil and notepad\n\n### What to Measure\n\n**Floor plan dimensions:**\n- Measure each wall from corner to corner at counter height (36")\n- Note the location and width of every window and door opening\n- Note which direction doors swing (this affects cabinet placement)\n- Measure the distance from the floor to the bottom of any windows (this determines upper cabinet height)\n- Note the ceiling height\n\n**Existing features to document:**\n- Location of the sink (and which wall the plumbing is on)\n- Location of the range or cooktop\n- Location of the refrigerator\n- Location of electrical outlets, switches, and light fixtures\n- Location of any soffits or bulkheads above the cabinets\n\n**Pro tip:** Measure twice. Always double-check your numbers before placing an order.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Standard Cabinet Dimensions\n\nUnderstanding standard sizes helps you plan a layout that works without custom modifications.\n\n### Base Cabinets\n\n| Dimension | Standard Size |\n|---|---|\n| Height | 34.5" (plus countertop = 36" finished) |\n| Depth | 24" |\n| Width range | 9" to 48" (in 3" increments) |\n\n### Wall (Upper) Cabinets\n\n| Dimension | Standard Size |\n|---|---|\n| Height | 30", 36", or 42" |\n| Depth | 12" |\n| Width range | 9" to 48" (in 3" increments) |\n| Mounting height | 18" above countertop (standard) |\n\n### Tall / Pantry Cabinets\n\n| Dimension | Standard Size |\n|---|---|\n| Height | 84", 90", or 96" |\n| Depth | 24" (pantry) or 12" (oven surround) |\n| Width range | 18" to 36" |\n\n### Countertop Overhang\nStandard countertop overhangs the base cabinet by **1.5"** on the front and sides (except against walls).\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: The Work Triangle\n\nThe kitchen work triangle is the relationship between the three primary work zones: the **refrigerator**, the **sink**, and the **range**. An efficient kitchen keeps these three points close enough to minimize walking but far enough apart to allow two people to work comfortably.\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Each leg of the triangle should be between **4 and 9 feet**\n- The total perimeter of the triangle should be between **13 and 26 feet**\n- No major traffic path should cross through the triangle\n\nIn open-plan kitchens and galley layouts, the triangle concept adapts — but the principle of keeping the three work zones accessible to each other remains the same.\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: Common Kitchen Layouts\n\n| Layout | Best For | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Galley** | Narrow spaces, efficiency | Two parallel runs of cabinets; excellent workflow |\n| **L-Shape** | Medium kitchens | Two adjacent walls; good corner use with lazy Susan or blind corner |\n| **U-Shape** | Large kitchens | Three walls; maximum storage; requires 8''+ width |\n| **G-Shape** | Large kitchens | U-shape with peninsula; great for two cooks |\n| **Single Wall** | Small spaces, studios | All cabinets on one wall; limited storage |\n| **Island** | Open-plan kitchens | Adds prep space and seating; needs 42"–48" clearance around it |\n\n---\n\n## Part 5: The 10×10 Kitchen — Your Baseline Reference\n\nThe industry standard for comparing cabinet pricing is the **10×10 kitchen**: two walls of cabinets, each 10 feet long, forming an L-shape.\n\n**A standard 10×10 kitchen includes:**\n- 12 base cabinets (various widths totaling 10 linear feet per wall)\n- 12 wall cabinets (matching the base layout)\n- No island, no specialty pieces, no trim or crown molding\n\nThis configuration is used as a baseline because it allows apples-to-apples price comparison between cabinet lines. When a customer asks "how much does a kitchen cost?" — start with the 10×10 as a reference point, then adjust for their actual layout.\n\n**What is NOT included in a 10×10 price:**\n- Crown molding and light rail\n- Lazy Susan or blind corner accessories\n- Pull-out trays and organizers\n- Tall / pantry cabinets\n- Island cabinets\n- Shipping\n\n---\n\n## Part 6: Upper Cabinet Height — 30" vs. 36" vs. 42"\n\nThe height of the upper cabinets dramatically affects the look and storage of the kitchen.\n\n| Height | Ceiling Clearance | Look | Storage |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 30" | 8'' ceiling with soffit, or 9''+ with gap | Traditional | Standard |\n| 36" | 9'' ceiling standard | Modern, spacious | More |\n| 42" | 9''–10'' ceiling | High-end, dramatic | Maximum |\n\n**For 8-foot ceilings:** 30" uppers with a soffit above, or 36" uppers that go to the ceiling (no soffit needed).\n\n**For 9-foot ceilings:** 42" uppers are the most popular choice — they reach near the ceiling and eliminate the dust-collecting gap.\n\n---\n\n## Part 7: Corner Solutions\n\nCorners are the most challenging part of any kitchen layout. There are four main options:\n\n**Blind corner base cabinet:** One cabinet extends into the corner; the other butts up against it. The "blind" section is accessible but requires reaching. Most economical option.\n\n**Lazy Susan:** A rotating shelf system inside a 33"–36" corner cabinet. Easy access, popular with homeowners.\n\n**Magic corner / Le Mans:** A pull-out system that brings items from the back of the corner to the front. Premium option, excellent accessibility.\n\n**Dead corner with drawer stack:** Fill the corner with a tall drawer stack and skip the corner cabinet entirely. Works well in modern kitchens.\n\n---\n\n## Part 8: Using the Cabinet Dealz Design Tool\n\nThe 2D design tool at cabinetdealz.com lets you plan a kitchen layout visually before ordering.\n\n**How to get the most from it:**\n1. Enter your room dimensions first — set the wall lengths accurately\n2. Place base cabinets along the walls, starting from corners\n3. Add upper cabinets above the base run\n4. Place the sink, range, and refrigerator to check the work triangle\n5. Switch between door styles to see how each one looks in the space\n6. Use the room scene photos to visualize the finished kitchen\n7. Save the design — it becomes the basis for your quote\n\n**Pro tip for affiliates:** Do the design together with the customer on a screen share. Walking them through the tool in real time is one of the most effective ways to close a sale — they become emotionally invested in the kitchen they just designed.\n\n---\n\n## Part 9: What to Order — A Quick Checklist\n\nBefore submitting any order, confirm the following with your customer:\n\n- [ ] All measurements verified (measured twice)\n- [ ] Door style selected\n- [ ] Finish/color confirmed\n- [ ] Corner solution decided (blind corner, lazy Susan, or other)\n- [ ] Sink base size confirmed (matches sink dimensions)\n- [ ] Range/cooktop base confirmed (matches appliance width)\n- [ ] Refrigerator panel or end panel needed?\n- [ ] Crown molding and light rail included?\n- [ ] Shipping address confirmed\n- [ ] Installation timeline confirmed\n\nA complete, accurate order avoids delays, returns, and unhappy customers.\n\n---\n\n*Cabinet Dealz Affiliate Program — cabinetdealz.com*\n', NULL, 'designer', NULL, 201, TRUE, FALSE FROM DUAL WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources WHERE type = 'guide' AND title = 'Kitchen Design Fundamentals');
-- guide · How to Use Your CRM Effectively
INSERT INTO cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources (type, title, body, url, category, metaJson, sortOrder, isPublished, featured) SELECT 'guide', 'How to Use Your CRM Effectively', '# How to Use Your CRM Effectively\n## Turn Your Pipeline Into Predictable Sales\n\n> Your CRM (Customer Relationship Management) system is the single most important tool for growing your cabinet business. Affiliates who use it consistently close more deals, waste less time, and never lose a lead to forgetfulness. This guide shows you exactly how to use it.\n\n---\n\n## What Is a CRM and Why Does It Matter?\n\nA CRM is a system for tracking every person you are selling to — where they are in the buying process, what you last said to them, and what your next step is. Without a CRM, leads fall through the cracks. With a CRM, every lead has a next action and a follow-up date.\n\nThe Cabinet Dealz CRM is built directly into your affiliate dashboard. You do not need any external software. Every lead you add is visible only to you.\n\n---\n\n## The 5 Pipeline Stages\n\nYour CRM uses a visual pipeline — a board with five columns, one for each stage of the sales process. Every lead (called a "deal") lives in exactly one stage at a time.\n\n| Stage | What It Means | What to Do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **New Lead** | You have their contact info but have not yet made contact | Reach out within 24 hours |\n| **Contacted** | You have spoken with them or sent your storefront link | Follow up in 3 days if no response |\n| **Quoted** | You have sent them a price or walked through the design tool | Follow up in 5–7 days |\n| **Negotiating** | They are interested but have questions, concerns, or are comparing options | Stay in contact every 3–5 days |\n| **Closed / Won** | They placed an order | Request a referral 2–3 weeks after delivery |\n\nThere is also a **Lost** status for leads who are no longer active. Do not delete lost leads — mark them lost and move on. They may come back in 6–12 months.\n\n---\n\n## How to Add a Lead\n\nAdd a new lead the moment you meet someone who might be interested — do not wait until you get home. The best time to add a lead is while the conversation is still fresh.\n\n**What to enter:**\n- **Name** — first and last\n- **Phone** — the number you will text or call\n- **Email** — for sending quotes and follow-ups\n- **Stage** — start at "New Lead" unless you have already made contact\n- **Notes** — anything relevant: "3-bedroom house, wants to update kitchen and two bathrooms, budget around $12,000, timeline 60 days"\n\n**The golden rule of CRM notes:** Write down everything you would want to remember if you had not spoken to this person in 30 days. Because in 30 days, you might not remember.\n\n---\n\n## The Follow-Up Cadence\n\nThe biggest mistake salespeople make is giving up too early. Research consistently shows that most sales require 5 to 12 contacts before a decision is made. Most salespeople give up after 2.\n\nYour CRM makes it easy to stay in contact systematically without being annoying.\n\n**Recommended cadence by stage:**\n\n**New Lead → Contacted:**\n- Contact within 24 hours of adding them\n- If no response, follow up at day 3 and day 7\n- If still no response, move to a monthly check-in\n\n**Contacted → Quoted:**\n- Send the storefront link and design tool invitation within 24 hours\n- Follow up 3 days later: "Did you get a chance to look at the site?"\n- Follow up 7 days later with a room scene photo or style suggestion\n\n**Quoted → Negotiating:**\n- Follow up 5 days after sending the quote\n- Ask specifically: "Do you have any questions about the quote?"\n- Address any objections (see the 10 Steps to a Sale guide)\n\n**Negotiating → Closed:**\n- Follow up every 3–5 days\n- Each contact should add value: a new photo, a customer story, an answer to a question\n- Ask for the order directly at least once per week\n\n---\n\n## Writing Good Notes\n\nYour notes are the memory of your sales process. A good note tells you exactly what happened and what to do next.\n\n**Bad note:** "Called, no answer"\n\n**Good note:** "Called at 2pm, no answer. Left voicemail. Will text tomorrow. She mentioned last week she was waiting on her contractor to confirm the start date — check back on that."\n\n**After every contact, write:**\n1. What you did (called, texted, met in person)\n2. What they said\n3. What the next step is and when\n\n---\n\n## Your Weekly CRM Review\n\nSet aside 15 minutes every Monday morning to review your pipeline. This is the most valuable 15 minutes of your sales week.\n\n**Ask yourself for each deal:**\n- What stage is this in?\n- When did I last contact them?\n- What is my next step?\n- Is there anything blocking this deal from moving forward?\n\nMove any deal that has been in the same stage for more than 2 weeks to the top of your priority list. Stalled deals need attention.\n\n---\n\n## Your Monthly Pipeline Report\n\nOnce a month, look at your pipeline from a higher level:\n\n- How many deals are in each stage?\n- How many deals did I close this month?\n- How many new leads did I add?\n- What is my conversion rate from Contacted to Closed?\n\nIf you are adding lots of leads but few are closing, the problem is in your follow-up or your close. Go back to Steps 7 and 8 of the 10 Steps to a Sale guide.\n\nIf you are not adding enough new leads, the problem is in your prospecting. Go back to Step 1.\n\n---\n\n## Using the CRM to Track Referrals\n\nEvery closed deal is an opportunity for a referral. Add a reminder in the CRM to follow up with every customer 2–3 weeks after their order is delivered.\n\n**Note to add at close:** "Order placed [date]. Follow up [date + 3 weeks] to check on delivery and ask for referrals."\n\nWhen you follow up, ask directly: "Do you know anyone else who is thinking about updating their kitchen? I would love an introduction."\n\nOne referral from a happy customer is worth more than 10 cold outreach attempts.\n\n---\n\n## CRM Best Practices — Quick Reference\n\n| Do | Don''t |\n|---|---|\n| Add every lead immediately | Wait until later to add leads |\n| Write detailed notes after every contact | Leave notes blank or vague |\n| Set a next action for every deal | Leave deals without a next step |\n| Review your pipeline every Monday | Only look at it when you remember |\n| Follow up 5–12 times before giving up | Give up after 2 attempts |\n| Mark lost deals as lost (don''t delete) | Delete leads who don''t buy immediately |\n| Ask for referrals from every closed deal | Forget to follow up after the sale |\n\n---\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nYour CRM is only as powerful as the habits you build around it. Affiliates who add every lead, write detailed notes, and follow up systematically consistently outperform those who rely on memory and good intentions. The system works — but only if you use it.\n\nAffiliates who follow the full system — including consistent CRM use — report a **35% increase in sales** compared to those who do not. That is not a coincidence. It is the result of never letting a lead go cold.\n\n---\n\n*Cabinet Dealz Affiliate Program — cabinetdealz.com*\n', NULL, 'crm', NULL, 202, TRUE, FALSE FROM DUAL WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources WHERE type = 'guide' AND title = 'How to Use Your CRM Effectively');
-- guide · Marketing Scripts & Templates
INSERT INTO cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources (type, title, body, url, category, metaJson, sortOrder, isPublished, featured) SELECT 'guide', 'Marketing Scripts & Templates', '# Marketing Scripts & Templates\n## Ready-to-Use Content for Cabinet Dealz Affiliates\n\n> Copy, customize with your store name and link, and use immediately. These templates are designed to generate leads, drive traffic to your storefront, and keep your pipeline full.\n\n---\n\n## Text Message Scripts\n\n### First Contact — Warm Lead (Someone You Know)\n> "Hey [Name]! I just launched my own cabinet store — [Your Store Name]. We carry beautiful kitchen cabinets at contractor pricing. If you or anyone you know is thinking about updating their kitchen, I''d love to help. Here''s my store: [your storefront URL]"\n\n### First Contact — Cold Outreach (Contractor / Builder)\n> "Hi [Name], I''m [Your Name] with [Your Store Name]. I supply quality kitchen cabinets to contractors and builders at competitive pricing. I''d love to be your go-to cabinet source. Can I send you my catalog? [your storefront URL]"\n\n### Follow-Up After No Response (Day 3)\n> "Hey [Name], just wanted to make sure you got my message! Here''s my store link again: [your storefront URL]. We have a 2D design tool so you can plan your whole kitchen before you order. No pressure — just wanted you to have it."\n\n### Sending the Design Tool\n> "I wanted to share something cool — there''s a free kitchen design tool on my site where you can lay out your whole kitchen and see different door styles. Takes about 10 minutes and gives you a real price. Want to try it? [your storefront URL]"\n\n### After Sending a Quote\n> "Hey [Name], I sent over the quote for your kitchen. Let me know if you have any questions or want to tweak anything. I''m happy to jump on a call and walk through it with you."\n\n### Referral Request (After Delivery)\n> "Hey [Name]! Hope the kitchen is looking amazing! If you know anyone else thinking about updating their kitchen, I''d love an introduction. Just forward them my store link: [your storefront URL] — I''ll take great care of them!"\n\n---\n\n## Email Templates\n\n### Introduction Email\n**Subject:** Beautiful Kitchen Cabinets — Contractor Pricing, No Contractor Required\n\n> Hi [Name],\n>\n> My name is [Your Name] and I run [Your Store Name], a cabinet store specializing in quality kitchen cabinets at pricing most homeowners never get access to.\n>\n> I work directly with a national cabinet network to offer contractor-grade cabinets — the same quality you''d see in a high-end remodel — at prices that are typically 20–40% less than big box stores.\n>\n> You can browse our full selection and use our free kitchen design tool here: [your storefront URL]\n>\n> If you''re thinking about updating your kitchen — or know someone who is — I''d love to help. Reply to this email or call/text me at [your phone number].\n>\n> [Your Name]\n> [Your Store Name]\n> [your storefront URL]\n\n### Follow-Up Email (After No Response)\n**Subject:** Quick follow-up — [Your Store Name]\n\n> Hi [Name],\n>\n> I wanted to follow up on my last message. I know life gets busy!\n>\n> If you''re still thinking about your kitchen, I''d love to put together a free quote for you. All I need is your kitchen dimensions and your preferred door style — and I can have a price to you within 24 hours.\n>\n> Here''s my store if you want to browse styles first: [your storefront URL]\n>\n> No pressure at all — just here when you''re ready.\n>\n> [Your Name]\n\n---\n\n## Social Media Captions\n\n### Facebook — General Awareness\n> 🏠 Thinking about updating your kitchen? I can help!\n>\n> I run [Your Store Name] — a cabinet store offering beautiful, quality kitchen cabinets at prices most homeowners never see. We carry dozens of door styles from classic white shaker to modern slab, and our free online design tool lets you plan your whole kitchen before you spend a dollar.\n>\n> 👉 Browse styles and get a free quote: [your storefront URL]\n>\n> Questions? Drop them in the comments or send me a message!\n\n### Facebook — Before/After (When You Have a Photo)\n> 🔥 Kitchen transformation alert!\n>\n> This customer came to me with a dated kitchen and a budget. We designed their new layout together using our online tool, ordered the cabinets, and look at the result!\n>\n> If your kitchen needs a refresh, let''s talk. I''ll help you design it, price it, and make it happen.\n>\n> 👉 [your storefront URL]\n\n### Facebook — Design Tool Feature\n> Did you know you can design your entire kitchen online — for free — before buying a single cabinet?\n>\n> Our 2D design tool lets you:\n> ✅ Enter your kitchen dimensions\n> ✅ Place cabinets exactly where you want them\n> ✅ See different door styles and colors\n> ✅ Get a real price instantly\n>\n> No salesperson pressure. No showroom appointment. Just you, your kitchen, and a plan.\n>\n> Try it here: [your storefront URL]\n\n### Instagram Caption\n> Kitchen goals 🤍 Swipe to see the door styles we carry — from classic shaker to sleek modern slab. Every style is available in multiple colors and finishes.\n>\n> 📐 Free design tool on our site — plan your whole kitchen in 10 minutes.\n> 💰 Contractor pricing. No contractor required.\n>\n> Link in bio → [your storefront URL]\n>\n> #kitchenremodel #kitchencabinets #kitchendesign #homeimprovement #kitchenrenovation #cabinetry #dreamkitchen\n\n### Instagram — Contractor/Trade\n> Contractors and builders — let''s talk. 🔨\n>\n> I supply quality kitchen cabinets to trade professionals at pricing that protects your margin. Fast quotes, reliable delivery, and a design tool your clients can use to visualize the finished kitchen.\n>\n> DM me or visit: [your storefront URL]\n>\n> #contractor #builder #kitchenremodel #cabinetry #tradeprofessional #homebuilder\n\n---\n\n## Google Business Post Templates\n\n### New Post — Monthly\n> [Your Store Name] is your local source for quality kitchen cabinets at contractor pricing. Browse dozens of door styles, use our free online design tool, and get a quote in 24 hours. Visit our online store: [your storefront URL]\n\n### Seasonal Post — Spring Remodel Season\n> Spring is the perfect time to finally tackle that kitchen remodel! [Your Store Name] makes it easy — browse styles online, design your layout with our free tool, and get a competitive quote fast. Start your project today: [your storefront URL]\n\n---\n\n## Phone Strip / Bulletin Board Text\n\n*(Use this text for your printed phone strip — see the Marketing Hub to download your personalized version)*\n\n> **[Your Store Name]**\n> Kitchen Cabinets — Contractor Pricing\n> [Your Phone Number]\n> Scan for my store → [QR code]\n\n---\n\n## Business Card Back Text\n\n*(The back of your business card — see the Marketing Hub to download your personalized version)*\n\n> **Scan to Shop My Store**\n> [QR code]\n> Browse 60+ door styles\n> Free kitchen design tool\n> Competitive pricing — no showroom required\n> [your storefront URL]\n\n---\n\n*Cabinet Dealz Affiliate Program — cabinetdealz.com*\n', NULL, 'marketing', NULL, 203, TRUE, FALSE FROM DUAL WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cabinet_dealz.affiliate_resources WHERE type = 'guide' AND title = 'Marketing Scripts & Templates');
